<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌿 LIVE CHLOROPHYLL / ALGAL ACTIVITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/chlorophyll/legend.png?v=central-noaacwN20VIIRSchlaDaily-v9-2026-10-04" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Carlson Trophic State Index from chlorophyll-a: 21 fixed preset table colors — <b>0-35 Oligotrophic</b> (chl 0.04-1.62) &rarr; <b>40-45 Mesotrophic</b> (chl 2.6-4.1) &rarr; <b>50-60 Eutrophic</b> (chl 6.4-20) &rarr; <b>65-100 Hypereutrophic</b> (chl 31-1,183). Every pixel shows its table row's exact color; the legend is the full table. High TSI = biomass / activity, not toxins.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE (daily composite)</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA CoastWatch S-NPP+NOAA-20 VIIRS chlorophyll-a NRT gapfilled Daily (ERDDAP nesdisVHNnoaaSNPPnoaa20NRTchlaGapfilledDaily), fallback SQ Daily (nesdisVHNSQchlaDaily); host fallback NOAA-20 VIIRS chlorophyll Daily on the CoastWatch Central hub (noaacwN20VIIRSchlaDaily, same chlor_a OC3 variable)<br/><a href="https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/nesdisVHNnoaaSNPPnoaa20NRTchlaGapfilledDaily.html">https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/nesdisVHNnoaaSNPPnoaa20NRTchlaGapfilledDaily.html</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE CHLOROPHYLL / ALGAL ACTIVITY</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/chlorophyll/legend.png?v=central-noaacwN20VIIRSchlaDaily-v9-2026-10-04" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Carlson Trophic State Index from chlorophyll-a: 21 fixed preset table colors — <b>0-35 Oligotrophic</b> (chl 0.04-1.62) &rarr; <b>40-45 Mesotrophic</b> (chl 2.6-4.1) &rarr; <b>50-60 Eutrophic</b> (chl 6.4-20) &rarr; <b>65-100 Hypereutrophic</b> (chl 31-1,183). Every pixel shows its table row's exact color; the legend is the full table. High TSI = biomass / activity, not toxins.</p><p>Chlorophyll-a concentration at the lake surface, a proxy for phytoplankton abundance/biomass, shown on the Carlson Trophic State Index 0-100. NOT a toxin measurement and NOT a harmful-algal-bloom diagnosis.</p><p><b>Units:</b> TSI (Carlson TSI from chlorophyll-a)<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA CoastWatch S-NPP+NOAA-20 VIIRS chlorophyll-a NRT gapfilled Daily (ERDDAP nesdisVHNnoaaSNPPnoaa20NRTchlaGapfilledDaily), fallback SQ Daily (nesdisVHNSQchlaDaily); host fallback NOAA-20 VIIRS chlorophyll Daily on the CoastWatch Central hub (noaacwN20VIIRSchlaDaily, same chlor_a OC3 variable)<br/><b>Update:</b> daily composites<br/><b>Why high values turn red/purple:</b> Carlson TSI trophic scale: oligotrophic blues through mesotrophic yellows and eutrophic oranges, with only hypereutrophic water reaching red-violet/deep-purple.<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/nesdisVHNnoaaSNPPnoaa20NRTchlaGapfilledDaily.html">ERDDAP dataset</a></p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>🌿 LIVE CHLOROPHYLL / ALGAL ACTIVITY</name>
        <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
        <Icon>
          <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/chlorophyll/current.png?v=central-noaacwN20VIIRSchlaDaily-v9-2026-10-04</href>
          <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
          <refreshInterval>21600</refreshInterval>
        </Icon>
        <LatLonBox>
          <north>49.5</north>
          <south>40.5</south>
          <east>-73.5</east>
          <west>-93.0</west>
        </LatLonBox>
      </GroundOverlay>
    </Folder>
  </Document>
</kml>
